1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,480
I’m standing on Blackheath Common in south-east
London. But if I wanted to tell someone my exact

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:11,800
location, how would I describe it? I could say
I was 500 metres south of Greenwich Observatory,

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:16,120
but that would only be useful to people who
know where the observatory is. I could use

4
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:20,360
the address of the nearest building, but that’s
several hundred metres away, so that wouldn’t be

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:26,920
very accurate. One reliable way to describe
where I am is to use a pair of co-ordinates.

6
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:31,480
Co-ordinates are numbers that specify a
location on the earth’s surface relative

7
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:36,720
to an agreed reference point. There are
lots of different co-ordinate systems,

8
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:40,440
but probably the best known is the
geographic co-ordinate system, which

9
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:47,240
uses latitude and longitude. That’s the system
underlying every GPS-enabled device you own.

10
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:52,640
Latitude is a measure of how far north
or south of the equator a point is,

11
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:56,720
while longitude is a measure of
how far east or west a point is,

12
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:02,204
relative to an imaginary line drawn through the
Greenwich Observatory, which is just over there.

13
00:01:02,204 --> 00:01:02,280
At the moment I’m standing exactly on
that line, so my longitude is zero.

14
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:07,280
The geographic co-ordinate system is
used in lots of applications. But one

15
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:12,360
difficulty with latitude and longitude is
that they are usually measured in degrees.

16
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:19,080
A latitude of zero means a location
on the equator, while a latitude of

17
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:22,498
plus ninety degrees means a location at the north
pole. Right now I’m standing at fifty-one degrees

18
00:01:22,498 --> 00:01:28,080
north of the equator. But degrees aren’t very
useful for measuring everyday distances – it’s

19
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:35,760
not very informative to say that the nearest
railway station is 0.01 degrees walk from here.

20
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:41,160
Because degrees of latitude and longitude
aren’t easy to work with, it’s often easier

21
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:47,240
to use a different type of coordinate system when
making maps. These are called projected coordinate

22
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:52,960
systems, and they work by specifying a location
relative to a well-defined starting point.

23
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:59,280
There are lots of different projected coordinate
systems that cover different parts of the Earth’s

24
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:05,320
surface. For example, for locations in London
we can use the British National Grid system.

25
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:11,120
This specifies locations based on how
many metres east and north they are from

26
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:14,960
a specific point in the Atlantic Ocean
off the coast of Cornwall. Because the

27
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:20,440
British National Grid measures distances
in metres, it is easier to work with.

28
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:25,280
We will use co-ordinates specified in lots
of projected co-ordinate systems during this

29
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:30,240
course. But there is one draw-back
of projected co-ordinate systems:

30
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:35,160
the people who design them have to deal with
the fact that maps are flat but the surface of

31
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:41,120
the earth is curved. We don’t need to go into
the mathematical details of how this is done,

32
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:47,760
but it’s important to know that there is simply
no way to accurately represent a curved surface

33
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:54,160
on a flat map without some distortion. There
are lots of different ways to manage this

34
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:59,760
problem. Which solution is best depends on the
circumstances, so people designing projected

35
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:04,920
co-ordinate systems for different parts of
the globe will make different decisions.

36
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:10,120
That has two consequences for us as map makers.
Firstly, we must know which co-ordinate system

37
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:16,240
a particular dataset uses. Knowing a pair of
co-ordinates is useless unless we also know

38
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:23,160
the co-ordinate system. Most spatial datasets have
this information embedded in them, but if not then

39
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:29,480
it’s vital that you find out what co-ordinate
system the data uses. One way to do that is to

40
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:35,560
ask whoever provided the data what co-ordinate
system they used. The second important point is

41
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:41,320
that projected co-ordinate systems only work for
the part of the globe that they were designed for.

42
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:45,840
If you use the wrong co-ordinate system, or
use a co-ordinate system for a different part

43
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:50,120
of the world, it’s quite likely that
your map will have errors in it or

44
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:55,200
some of the features will look distorted.
For example, you probably recognise this

45
00:03:55,200 --> 00:04:01,320
outline of the UK. But if you told a computer
to transform that outline using a co-ordinate

46
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:08,080
system designed to make maps of Canada,
you’ll see the outline becomes deformed.

47
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:13,760
Dealing with co-ordinates can seem complicated,
but they allow us to accurately identify locations

48
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:18,760
on the earth’s surface. That’s a crucial
step in being able to do useful things with

49
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:25,280
spatial data – we will learn about many of
those things during the rest of the course.

50
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,520
In summary:

51
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:31,680
We use co-ordinates to describe
locations on the Earth’s surface

52
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:36,880
Different projected co-ordinate systems
are used for different parts of the globe

53
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:45,680
It is vital that we know which co-ordinate
system a particular dataset uses